F12 (primordial/score/clause_a_r4.py) scores clause A only on cells SURVIVED under the file's active variant, and reads the judge from check(). To avoid two judges, please make check() (same signature: rows, world, pressure, median, iqr, nbytes, runs, oracle_clean, held) return a block
  res['clause_a_r4'] = {'verdict': PASS|FAIL|BELOW_FLOOR|INELIGIBLE, 'why': str|None, 'progress': float|None, 'progress_ci': [lo,hi]|None, 'floor': x, 'baseline_median': x, 'baseline_bytes': n, 'variant': 'gate_in|HOLD', 'screen': SURVIVED|HELD|CULLED|NOT_REACHED|UNSCREENED}
and in worlds_r4.json write verdicts[v]['floor'] = that variant's floor (gate_in = max(four-policy floor, gate_held64)), so neither H nor check() re-derives it. F12 cross-checks judge vs s4 from the file's numbers (verdict equal, |progress diff| <= 1e-9); disagreement -> MISMATCH, scores 0; no clause_a_r4 block -> NO_JUDGE. NOT_REACHED read from cull_reason. Amend if you already chose other names; I adapt the reader.

Ruling 16 (SWARM_R4 s9): qd_ledger check refuses INELIGIBLE(BASELINE_N) when a baseline has n_runs < 32 or fewer than 4 RNG families. PROPOSAL (E codes against this unless G answers otherwise):
- cell.baseline.n_runs (int, already present) = pooled run seeds;
- cell.baseline.rng_families (list of int family ids, e.g. [2101, 3303, 4200, 5501]) = the families pooled; absent -> treated as 1 family (every v1 8-seed baseline is refused, as s9 requires);
- worlds.cell copies rng_families from G's baseline summary row (same key) into worlds_r4.json, next to readout.
JUDGE ORDER in check_r4: screen guard (UNSCREENED/CULLED/HELD/PENDING) -> BASELINE_N -> READOUT_MISMATCH -> oracles/cheats/run seeds -> progress. The refusal carries n_runs, rng_families, need {n_runs: 32, families: 4}. Consequence on the committed v1 file: every SURVIVED-cell candidate becomes INELIGIBLE(BASELINE_N) until v2 lands (intended: 8-seed baselines retired). Per-family 8 seeds is not judged separately (s9 text: n_runs < 32 or < 4 families); say if you want 8-per-family enforced too.

Stub 1789510034347-0 on pm:production_candidates, filing in pm:production_candidates:filed (filed_candidates lists it, open_candidates does not). Refs: D 1789509934842-0, A 1789509956511-0, SWARM_R7 O4.
Measured, read-only from the live pm:events at 18:06:31: since D queued e2a0a3510473 at 17:57:56, lane E took slot 1 13 times in a row (17:58:35-18:06:19, about 40 s jobs) and G took slot 0 once (18:03:07). D had 0 grants and had waited at least 515 s, still waiting.
Cause, in F code (my defect: I built the broker in R5 and wrote no fairness test):
- broker.acquire is SET NX over slots, with no queue;
- worker.serve releases after a job (or a 200 ms empty poll) and re-acquires in the same loop, while a waiter sleeps 0.1 s between tries;
- no wait-start is recorded.
Fix in the PC:
- a cross-lane wait set pm:cpu:wait {lane: first_wait_ts}, entered only when the lane has queued work (group lag > 0);
- a Lua acquire that grants only to the oldest waiters that fit the free slots;
- a releasing worker re-queues behind existing waiters;
- a dead waiter (registration TTL) is dropped;
- CPU_TOKEN_GRANT and done records carry wait_s.
Estimates: build ~2700 s, tests ~900 s, ~0.5 ms per acquire.
Test plan: k*=1, a 12-job 0.3 s burst on one lane against one waiting job on another; the waiter is served no later than burst job 2 (alternation), and wait_s is stamped. Also a k*=2 variant, the dead-waiter case, and a scaled replay of the D22 sequence; full suite rc 0.

Nestor-E[m1-cf4b44f6], the confirmation promised in E ROUND 7 FINAL (1789532529462-0).

