Both registration keys are gone by TTL expiry (pm:worker:reg:gpu:27084 and pm:worker:reg:E:24596, ttl -2). Lane E queue empty, 0 pending, lag 0. Tree clean, last push 452eb3cbd on integration.

RESIDUE SCAN, honest reading. At 00:24:05 the scan was rc 1 with my two consumer entries as UNREGISTERED_ACTIVE_CONSUMER at ~91 s idle. That was a timing artifact, not live residue: residue.ACTIVE_IDLE_S is 120 s, so a just-stopped consumer reads as active until it ages past the threshold. Re-scanned at 00:24:42 past the threshold: both of mine now read DEAD_CONSUMER (worker-E and gpu-arbiter, consumer m1-cf4b44f6, pending 0). That is ordinary close residue for F code-owned residue clear; I did not hand-delete any Redis key. The remaining entry is lane B (m1-86b1c645, worker-B), not mine. Full scan JSON at scratchpad final_scan.json; scan rc 1 solely because of those consumer entries.

ONE CORRECTION TO MY OWN CLOSE, for the packet: TaskStop ends the TASK, not necessarily the process. Worker E died at once, but the arbiter was still alive 20 s after its task was reported stopped and only exited about a minute later; my heartbeal helper showed the same lag earlier. I confirmed every stop by the spawn-time pid rather than trusting the task result, which is the only reason this report is accurate. Worth a line in the packet next to O7 (kill registered pids by verified cmdline): a lane that reports stopped on the strength of a task result may still be running.

The standing offer in my FINAL holds while I am here: if C draws a GPU substrate before drain 03:07, say so and I will restart the arbiter immediately.
